## 2026-11-18 — Verification re-review deferrals (tracked, not fixed)

Two findings from the verification re-review (/tmp/edi_r3_verification_gpt62sol.md)
are documented deferrals, not oversights:

- **Finding 13 — observation-quote provenance.** `run_evaluator` still returns the
  parsed observation without checking it against the artifact. The artifact fence
  (fresh per-call token, prompt-level defense) is implemented; whether the judge's
  observation is an EXACT artifact quote is a separate concern retained as a
  tracked deferral — a judge stub echoing the fence as its observation still passes
  the adapter. Deferral accepted by the Owner: prompt-level defense now,
  provenance check as its own change.
- **Finding 11 — legacy-log CWD fallback.** `custody_verify` retains the
  caller-directory fallback for pre-job-pin (legacy) logs BY DESIGN: those logs
  were written with keys relative to the caller and must still verify from the
  directory they were written in. Modern (job-pinned) logs refuse caller-relative
  resolution; conflicting legacy candidates are refused as ambiguous.

## 2026-11-18 — Final targeted fix round: two tracked known-limits (verification re-review 2)

- **Unified accounting journal — REJECTED (tracked known-limit).** The final-round
  finding that cross-references alone do not commit either ledger's newest tail is
  addressed by extending `Meter.anchor()` to commit BOTH ledger heads (usage AND
  reservation row count + head digest) at every state write, validated at the next
  `verify()`. A single durable accounting journal unifying the two ledgers was
  considered and rejected as out of scope for this round: the threat model is
  operator/machine-level file writes, and an adversary who can truncate or rewrite
  `usage.resv.jsonl` can rewrite `state.json` (and its anchor) just as easily — the
  journal adds no real assurance against that adversary, only complexity. The
  anchor extension closes the accidental-truncation gap; a deliberate full-rewrite
  adversary remains out of scope by the same rationale. Migration: recovery
  matches only usage rows carrying `reservation_id`; legacy rows without an id
  are anchored as-is (exact association cannot be safely inferred).
- **In-flight settlement after finalize — tracked known-limit (conservative
  accounting).** Closing admission is now checked by the metering proxy under the
  job-scoped flock BEFORE any paid call is forwarded, so cooperating flows cannot
  open paid work after a disposition. This is NOT a drain protocol: a call already
  in flight at the moment of finalize may settle after it. Its spend is still
  recorded by the reservation ledger (reserved before forwarding) and recovered at
  the next Meter construction — accounting stays conservative (spend is never
  lost, only recorded late).
